WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.500
 

00:00:01.210 --> 00:00:02.340
Hello.

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:07.200
After seven year old Philip
saw the Jurassic Park movie,

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.890
he had a dream of seeing
a real dinosaur one day.

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:16.330
The world's most famous paleontologist,
Jack Horner,

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:20.060
is now 70 years old, but
he has the same dream.

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:26.468
But how can we recreate the dinosaur
if we don't know its genome?

00:00:26.468 --> 00:00:32.850
Jack Horner was shy and
introverted when he was growing up.

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:35.320
He progressed so slowly in reading and

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:39.136
mathematics that other
kids called him dumb.

00:00:39.136 --> 00:00:44.740
However, his high school project on
dinosaurs won the science fair and

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:47.010
he was admitted to
the University of Montana.

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:52.850
However, after failing five
consecutive quarters, he dropped out.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:58.380
Fortunately for Horner,
he eventually found his calling.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:00.770
After working as a truck driver,

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:03.850
he accepted a job as
a technician at Princeton,

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:08.870
where he quickly established
a reputation as a brilliant researcher.

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:13.250
He would go on to become
an advisor to Steven Spielberg for

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:14.960
the Jurassic Park movie.

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:21.775
By that time, Horner had learned
that he suffers from dyslexia,

00:01:21.775 --> 00:01:26.525
a disorder that is characterized
by difficulty with mathematics.

00:01:26.525 --> 00:01:31.353
He was able to succeed despite
dyslexia because paleontologists

00:01:31.353 --> 00:01:33.642
hardly ever use mathematics.

00:01:33.642 --> 00:01:38.297
However, Horner's own
student would show that even

00:01:38.297 --> 00:01:42.236
paleontology is not immune from computing.

00:01:42.236 --> 00:01:48.491
15 years ago, Horner was exploring
his favorite Dinosaur graveyard and

00:01:48.491 --> 00:01:52.880
discovered a 68 million
years old T-Rex fossil.

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:57.060
He gave a chunk of this fossil
to his student, Mary Schweitzer,

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:01.670
who de-mineralized it and
sent it to mass spectrometrist John Asara.

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:10.030
In 2007, after analyzing thousands of
spectra, Asara, Horner, and Schweitzer

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:14.710
published a paper in science announcing
the discovery of T-Rex peptides.

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:22.470
Amazingly, these T-Rex peptides were
nearly identical to chicken peptides.

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:27.278
Thus supporting the controversial
hypothesis that birds evolved

00:02:27.278 --> 00:02:28.573
from dinosaurs.

00:02:28.573 --> 00:02:33.372
Horner even published a book
called "How to Build a Dinosaur,"

00:02:33.372 --> 00:02:39.219
to explain how to genetically modify
a chicken to re-create a dinosaur.

00:02:39.219 --> 00:02:42.850
Yet, some scientists remain skeptical.

00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:47.095
While previous dinosaur studies
did not require much computation,

00:02:47.095 --> 00:02:53.080
T-Rex analysis was powered by
a complex and error prone algorithm.

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:56.190
But how can we know which side is correct?

00:02:56.190 --> 00:03:01.480
Today, we will investigate the T-Rex
peptide by developing a protein,

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:04.410
rather than DNA, sequencing algorithm.

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:08.400
We have already talked
about Frederick Sanger and

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:13.440
his invention of DNA sequencing
technology four decades ago.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:18.913
Yet, Sanger had already won his first
Nobel prize six decades ago for

00:03:18.913 --> 00:03:23.520
determining the amino
acid sequence of insulin.

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:28.500
Similar to how scientists sequence genomes,
Sanger broke multiple

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:34.080
molecules of insulin into short peptides,
sequenced those peptides,

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:38.180
and then assembled them into
the amino acid sequence of insulin.

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:47.000
Also, protein sequencing was difficult in
the 1950s, but DNA sequencing was impossible.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:52.850
Today, DNA sequencing is trivial, but
protein sequencing remains difficult.

00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:58.060
That is why most proteins are discovered
by first sequencing a genome and

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:02.020
then predicting all of the genes
that this genome encodes.

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:07.500
By translating the nucleotide sequence
of each protein coding gene

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:14.130
into an amino acid sequence, biologists
derive a putative proteome of a species.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:21.420
However, different cells in an organism
express different proteins.

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:26.365
For example, brain cells express
proteins giving rise to neuropeptides.

00:04:26.365 --> 00:04:28.570
But kidney cells do not.

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:32.560
An important problem
in the study of proteins, or proteomics,

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:35.950
is to identify which specific proteins

00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:40.250
are present in each biological tissue,
and how they interact.

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:46.950
Today, instead of using Sanger's
old protein sequencing approach,

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:49.570
biologists use mass spectrometers:

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:53.990
Expensive and very accurate
molecular scales.

00:04:53.990 --> 00:04:58.160
But modern mass spectrometers
cannot read individual amino acids.

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:02.960
Instead, they generate a cryptic
fingerprint of each peptide,

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:05.160
called a "mass spectrum".

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:10.210
Our goal today is to
decode these fingerprints.

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:15.610
To analyze proteins, we need to
start breaking them into pieces and

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:18.500
measuring masses of
the resulting fragments,

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:20.170
using mass spectrometers, of course.

00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:25.460
Let's recall that different amino
acids have different masses.

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:32.051
For example, the mass of glycine is 57,
but the mass of alanine is 71.

00:05:32.051 --> 00:05:38.520
A mass spectrometer generally breaks
each protein molecule into two parts,

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:43.980
that we call "suffix" and "prefix" fragments,

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:45.290
and measures their masses.

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:50.776
It's important to realize that, when
biologists analyze samples, there

00:05:50.776 --> 00:05:56.300
are millions of different molecules of
the same peptides in the sample, and

00:05:56.300 --> 00:06:01.870
each of these peptides may break
individually over individual bonds.

00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:06.280
Mass spectrometers measure the
masses of all these fragments.

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:12.940
This simple scenario is a little
bit more complicated in practice

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:17.990
because, in reality,
most mass spectrometers can only measure

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:23.020
masses of relatively short fragments,
maybe 30-40 amino acid long.

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:30.170
To bypass this limitation,
the biologists usually use proteases,

00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:35.390
such as trypsin, to break proteins
into smaller pieces called "peptides".

00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:41.420
Afterwards, a mass spectrometer
breaks this peptide into

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:44.660
even smaller charged fragment ions and

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:50.680
measures the mass-to-charge ratio and
intensity of each ion.

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:55.300
Intensity is a proxy for
the number of fragments

00:06:55.300 --> 00:07:00.470
in the sample observed in the experiment.

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:03.780
Please note that, for
simplicity in this chapter,

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:08.940
we will assume that all masses
are integers and all charges are one.

00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:16.355
Our goal is to reconstruct the peptide
from this rather complex fingerprint.

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:22.920
Here is one of T-Rex's
spectra published in 2007.

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:28.521
Try to figure out what peptide
generated this spectrum, and

00:07:28.521 --> 00:07:33.330
you will learn something
about T-Rex proteins.

